; genus-bibliography.fk — the German literature on `der Mond` / `die Sonne` and what the
; gender means, with verification status as a first-class column.
;
; Asked 2026-07-28 by Urs: "is there a german book about `der Mond` and `die Sonne` that
; goes deeper into the gender meaning of those?"
;
; Yes — several, and this is the one question in the whole thread where answering from
; memory would have been the exact failure the rest of it argues against. Inventing a
; plausible German title and author is the cheapest lie available here and the hardest
; for a reader to catch. So every row below was looked up this session and its citation
; checked against a source, and the `verified` column says so per row rather than in a
; blanket sentence at the bottom.
;
; ── The one that answers the question most directly ─────────────────────────────────
;
; **Jacob Grimm, Deutsche Mythologie (1835), Kap. XXII "Himmel und Gestirne."**
; The deep German treatment of exactly this pair. Read this session; it carries the
; claim, the counter-evidence and the folk usage together:
;
;   "goth. mêna, ahd. mano, ags. mona, altn. mani überall männlich"
;   "sunne schwankt noch im mhd. auffallend zwischen männlichem und weiblichem genus"
;   "Das volk pflegte sich ... gern auszudrücken ›frau sonne‹, ›herr mond‹"
;   "da Sôl in der edda unter den asinnen aufgezählt erscheint"
;
; That second line is the one that costs me something. **The sun's gender wavered in
; Middle High German** — noticeably, between masculine and feminine, in Grimm's own
; words. My earlier cells presented the Germanic feminine sun as an inherited constant
; arriving unchanged. Grimm, in 1835, in the primary source, records it as unsettled for
; a stretch. The pattern is real and the stability was overstated, by me, and the
; correction comes from the book Urs asked for.
;
; ── The two positions, and they are still fighting ──────────────────────────────────
;
; Grimm also built the THEORY Urs has been circling: in Deutsche Grammatik Bd. III
; (1831) he held that grammatical gender is natural sex transferred onto all nouns by
; the early imagination — gender as personification. That is the position that makes a
; feminine sun mean something.
;
; Brugmann answered it: "Das Nominalgeschlecht in den indogermanischen Sprachen" (1889)
; and "Zur Frage der Entstehung des grammatischen Geschlechts" (1891) — gender as formal
; and morphological, not a reading of the referent. That is, roughly, the position this
; thread's derivation landed on, arrived at independently and about 137 years late.
;
; So the argument Urs and I have been having is a named argument with two sides and a
; date. Neither of us invented it.
;
; ── The modern German book that argues against my line ──────────────────────────────
;
; **Damaris Nübling, Genus und Geschlecht: Zum Zusammenhang von grammatischer,
; biologischer und sozialer Kategorisierung (Franz Steiner, 2020).** Her work finds
; close and complex relationships between genus and gender, and takes up exactly the
; cases this thread leaned on — neuter over female persons: `das Weib`, `das Mensch`,
; `das Anna`, `das Merkel`. That is `das Mädchen`'s neighbourhood, and she reads it as
; socially loaded rather than as the suffix being blind.
;
; It is named here BECAUSE it argues against the line I took, not despite that. Urs's
; original intuition has a serious German scholar behind it and he should have the
; reference. Also Nübling & Diewald (Hg.), Genus – Sexus – Gender (De Gruyter, 2022).
;
; And **Köpcke & Zubin, "Prinzipien für die Genuszuweisung im Deutschen"** (in: Lang &
; Zifonun (Hg.), Deutsch – typologisch, de Gruyter 1996, S. 473–491) — abstract read
; this session — argues explicitly AGAINST the arbitrariness thesis: German gender is
; "motivated by phonological, morphological and semantic principles." Semantic is in
; that list. My derivation ran the shape-vs-referent contrast hard; Köpcke and Zubin say
; the assignment system is motivated on several levels at once, semantics among them.
; The `-chen` result stands — it is morphological and it does override — but "the
; referent is never consulted" is too strong as a general statement about German gender
; assignment, and this is the citation that says so.
;
; ── The lane ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Every row's citation was checked against a retrieved source this session. What was NOT
; done is equally named: Grimm's Kap. XXII was read in quotation, not cover to cover;
; Köpcke & Zubin was read at the abstract, the PDF text did not extract; Nübling's book
; was verified as existing with title, publisher and year, and NOT read. So `verified`
; below means "the citation is real and checked", never "I have read the argument".
; Those are different claims and the column keeps them apart.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
; witnessed: 2026-07-28 -> 11111111, four-way

(do
    ; (author work year position citation-verified read-depth)
    ; position:  "personification" | "formal" | "motivated" | "genus-sexus-social"
    ; read-depth: 2 primary text read in quotation, 1 abstract/metadata only, 0 existence only
    (defn gb-row (author work year position verified depth)
        (list author work year position verified depth))
    (defn gb-author   (r) (nth r 0))
    (defn gb-work     (r) (nth r 1))
    (defn gb-year     (r) (nth r 2))
    (defn gb-position (r) (nth r 3))
    (defn gb-verified (r) (nth r 4))
    (defn gb-depth    (r) (nth r 5))

    (defn gb-rows ()
        (list
            (gb-row "Grimm"   "Deutsche Grammatik Bd. III"        1831 "personification"    1 1)
            (gb-row "Grimm"   "Deutsche Mythologie Kap. XXII"     1835 "personification"    1 2)
            (gb-row "Brugmann" "Das Nominalgeschlecht"            1889 "formal"             1 1)
            (gb-row "Brugmann" "Zur Frage der Entstehung"         1891 "formal"             1 1)
            (gb-row "Koepcke-Zubin" "Prinzipien der Genuszuweisung" 1996 "motivated"        1 1)
            (gb-row "Nuebling" "Genus und Geschlecht"             2020 "genus-sexus-social" 1 0)
            (gb-row "Nuebling-Diewald" "Genus - Sexus - Gender"   2022 "genus-sexus-social" 1 0)))

    (defn gb-all-verified? (rs)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) 1
            (if (eq (gb-verified (head rs)) 1) (gb-all-verified? (tail rs)) 0)))

    ; nothing here is asserted as read-in-full: the highest depth reached is 2, and only
    ; for the one chapter actually quoted
    (defn gb-max-depth (rs)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) 0
            (if (gt (gb-depth (head rs)) (gb-max-depth (tail rs)))
                (gb-depth (head rs)) (gb-max-depth (tail rs)))))
    (defn gb-count-depth (rs d)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) 0
            (add (if (eq (gb-depth (head rs)) d) 1 0) (gb-count-depth (tail rs) d))))

    ; how many distinct positions the literature holds — the question is live
    (defn gb-seen? (p ps)
        (if (eq (len ps) 0) 0 (if (str_eq p (head ps)) 1 (gb-seen? p (tail ps)))))
    (defn gb-pos-go (rs seen)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) (len seen)
            (if (eq (gb-seen? (gb-position (head rs)) seen) 1)
                (gb-pos-go (tail rs) seen)
                (gb-pos-go (tail rs) (cons (gb-position (head rs)) seen)))))
    (defn gb-positions () (gb-pos-go (gb-rows) (empty)))

    (defn gb-span () (sub 2022 1831))

    ; rows arguing AGAINST the line this thread took — counted, not buried
    (defn gb-against? (r)
        (if (str_eq (gb-position r) "personification") 1
            (if (str_eq (gb-position r) "genus-sexus-social") 1
                (if (str_eq (gb-position r) "motivated") 1 0))))
    (defn gb-against-count (rs)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) 0 (add (gb-against? (head rs)) (gb-against-count (tail rs)))))

    ; the correction the primary source handed back: the MHG sun wavered
    (defn gb-mhg-sun-wavered () 1)

    (defn gb-bit (cond bit) (if cond bit 0))
    (defn genus-bibliography-check ()
        (add (add (add (add (add (add (add
            ; 1 — every citation was checked against a retrieved source; none recalled
            (gb-bit (eq (gb-all-verified? (gb-rows)) 1) 1)
            ; 2 — and no row claims to have been read in full: depth tops out at 2
            (gb-bit (eq (gb-max-depth (gb-rows)) 2) 10))
            ; 3 — exactly one row reaches that depth, the chapter actually quoted
            (gb-bit (eq (gb-count-depth (gb-rows) 2) 1) 100))
            ; 4 — two rows are existence-verified only, and say so rather than implying more
            (gb-bit (eq (gb-count-depth (gb-rows) 0) 2) 1000))
            ; 5 — the literature holds several distinct positions: the question is live
            (gb-bit (gt (gb-positions) 2) 10000))
            ; 6 — and has been for the better part of two centuries
            (gb-bit (gt (gb-span) 150) 100000))
            ; 7 — most rows argue AGAINST the line this thread took. Counted, not buried
            (gb-bit (gt (gb-against-count (gb-rows))
                        (sub (len (gb-rows)) (gb-against-count (gb-rows)))) 1000000))
            ; 8 — and the primary source handed back a correction: the MHG sun wavered
            ;     between masculine and feminine, so the inherited-constant claim was mine
            ;     and overstated
            (gb-bit (eq (gb-mhg-sun-wavered) 1) 10000000)))
    0)
